To configure Laravel Dusk on an AWS EC2 instance, follow these steps:

1. Install Laravel: If you haven't already, install Laravel on your AWS EC2 instance by following the official documentation: https://laravel.com/docs/8.x/installation

2. Create a new Laravel project: Inside the Laravel root directory, run the following command:

   ```
   php artisan new-project my-project-name
   ```

3. Install Dusk: Inside your newly created Laravel project, require and install Laravel Dusk using Composer:

   ```
   composer require --dev laravel/dusk
   ```

4. Set up Dusk: In your Laravel project's `config/app.php` file, register the Dusk service provider:

   ```php
   'providers' => [
       // other providers...

       Laravel\Dusk\DuskServiceProvider::class,
   ],

   'aliases' => [
       // other aliases...

       'Dusk' => Laravel\Dusk\Facades\Dusk::class,
   ],

   'paths' => [
       'tests' => base_path('tests'),
   ],
   ```

5. Generate the Dusk authentication credentials: Run the following command to generate the necessary Dusk authentication credentials:

   ```
   php artisan dusk:authenticate
   ```

6. Configure Dusk: Inside your Laravel project's `config/dusk.php` file, configure the Dusk settings according to your needs, including the executable path for your preferred browser. For example:

   ```php
   'driver' => env('DUSK_DRIVER', 'chrome'),

   'launchOptions' => [
       'args' => ['--no-sandbox', '--disable-setuid-sandbox'],
   ],
   ```

   Note: The `args` key is an example configuration for running Chrome with the `--no-sandbox` and `--disable-setuid-sandbox` flags. Check the official documentation for a complete list of browser-specific options: https:// GitHub.com/laravel/dusk/blob/8.x/src/StandaloneBrowser.php#L92

7. Write your tests: Create a new file inside the newly created `tests/Dusk` directory, such as `ExampleTest.php`, and write your test using the `@dusk` attribute:

   ```php
   <?php

   namespace Tests\Browser;

   use App\User;
   use Tests\DuskTestCase;
   use Laravel\Dusk\Browser;
   use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\WithFaker;
   use Illuminate\ contract\Auth;

   class ExampleTest extends DuskTestCase
   {
       /**
        * A basic feature test example.
        *
        * @return void
        */
       public function testBasicExample()
       {
           $this->browse(static function (Browser $browser) {
               $browser->visit('https://example.com')
                       ->assertSee('Example Domain');

               $this->loginAsUser($browser);
               $browser->visit('/dashboard')
                       ->assertSee('Dashboard');
           });
       }

       /**
        * Log in a user.
        *
        * @param Browser $browser
        * @return void
        */
       protected function loginAsUser($browser)
       {
           $user = User::factory()->create();

           $browser->visit('/login')
                   ->type('email', $user->email)
                   ->type('password', 'password')
                   ->press('Login')
                   ->assertAuthenticated();
       }
   }
   ```

   Note: In this example, we assume that you have a `User` model defined, with an email and a default password. Adjust the `loginAsUser()` method to match your setup.

8. Start the test suite: Run the following command to start your Dusk test suite:

   ```
   php artisan dusk
   ```

9. Troubleshooting: If you encounter issues while running your Dusk tests, check the following resources for help:

   - Laravel documentation: https://laravel.com/docs/8.x/dusk#troubleshooting
   - Stack Overflow: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/dusk-laravel

